From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/base/arm: fix external-module include path on kernels where $(srctree) is the kernel tree

drivers/base/arm/Kbuild computes the include path for its own
top-level headers (include/linux/version_compat_defs.h,
include/linux/protected_memory_allocator.h) with:

    src:=$(if $(patsubst /%,,$(src)),$(srctree)/$(src),$(src))
    ccflags-y += -I$(src)/../../../include

This is a legacy compatibility shim from ARM's upstream mali_kbase
tree that assumes when $(src) is relative (".", the non-absolute
case), prefixing it with $(srctree) reconstructs the absolute path to
THIS module's own directory. That assumption does not hold against
the kernel tree used here (Linux v7.2-rc4 mainline): $(srctree) is the
KERNEL source tree root, not this out-of-tree module's directory, so
the computed include path resolves to a location three directories
above the *kernel* source tree instead of three directories above
drivers/base/arm within the GPU driver's own source tree. The result:
memory_group_manager.c and protected_memory_allocator.c fail to build
with

    fatal error: linux/version_compat_defs.h: No such file or directory
    fatal error: linux/protected_memory_allocator.h: No such file or directory

$(M) (aka KBUILD_EXTMOD) is set by Kbuild to the absolute path this
external module was invoked with (`make -C $(KDIR) M=<this dir>
modules`) regardless of kernel version or $(srctree) semantics, so use
that instead of the fragile $(srctree)-based reconstruction. This
matches the actual invocation in gpu.mk
(`$(MAKE) -C $(MGM_SRC_PATH) ...`, where MGM_SRC_PATH =
$(CIX_GPU_PATH)/drivers/base/arm becomes $(M) for the sub-make), so
$(M)/../../../include always resolves to $(CIX_GPU_PATH)/include, the
correct location, independent of kernel version.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/base/arm/Kbuild
+++ b/drivers/base/arm/Kbuild
@@ -25,8 +25,12 @@
 #
 # ccflags
 #
-src:=$(if $(patsubst /%,,$(src)),$(srctree)/$(src),$(src))
-ccflags-y += -I$(src)/../../../include
+# Use $(M) (KBUILD_EXTMOD), not $(srctree)-relative $(src): on kernels
+# where $(srctree) resolves to the kernel tree rather than this
+# out-of-tree module's own tree, the old $(srctree)-based
+# reconstruction points at the wrong directory. $(M) is always this
+# module's absolute invocation directory regardless of kernel version.
+ccflags-y += -I$(M)/../../../include

 subdir-ccflags-y += $(ccflags-y)

